ratelimit (1.1.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: the same source port can again be defined for both TCP and UDP
    on one command line (e.g. DNS-style setups, port ranges per
    protocol). Duplicate-listener detection ran while rules were being
    created, before post-hoc flags such as `-p udp` had been applied,
    so the second pair was rejected as a duplicate of the first.
    Deduplication now happens once, after CLI parsing and config-file
    merging are complete; genuine duplicates are still dropped with a
    warning that names the rule being kept.
  * Fix: stdout is now line-buffered when not attached to a terminal, so
    the startup rule table and the monitor appear immediately in
    journalctl / log files instead of being held in the stdio buffer.

 -- Alejandro M. <ale@manalejandro.com>  Fri, 30 Oct 2026 20:30:00 +0000

ratelimit (1.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New upstream release.
    - Feature: port-range mapping on the command line and in the config
      file (--src HOST:8000-8010 --dst HOST:9000-9010).
    - Fix: token-bucket deadlock when a received chunk exceeded the
      bucket capacity (rates below 64 kB/s could hang a connection
      forever); recv chunks are now clamped to the bucket size.
    - Fix: use-after-free on SIGHUP reload — shared security/monitor
      state is now heap-allocated and refcounted, so in-flight
      connections drain safely; reloads validate the new config before
      tearing anything down and no longer kill established connections.
    - Fix: UDP epoll registrations pointed at array slots moved by
      swap-remove, which could deliver replies to the wrong client or
      touch closed sockets; sessions are now stable heap nodes.
    - Fix: UDP session teardown loop leaked half of the sessions
      (file descriptors) on stop/reload.
    - Fix: signal race between the main loop and pause() could hang
      shutdown forever; signals are now consumed synchronously via
      sigwait().
    - Fix: per-rule max_connections was silently ignored (security
      context was initialised from global_max_connections/rule_count).
    - Fix: automatic blacklisting was unreachable dead code; it now
      triggers after 3 consecutive per-IP rejections.
    - Fix: the per-IP limit did not apply to UDP (slot taken/released
      per packet); it is now held for the lifetime of a session.
    - Fix: global_max_connections was never enforced; it now caps TCP
      connections plus UDP sessions.
    - Fix: idle timeout killed connections that were active in one
      direction; idleness is now tracked across both directions, and
      EINTR no longer tears connections down.
    - Fix: DNS resolution and blocking connect() ran in the accept
      loop, letting a dead destination stall a whole rule (DoS); they
      now happen per-connection with a 5 s timeout.
    - Fix: out-of-bounds read parsing -n/--name (memcpy of 63 bytes
      from argv) and the config section header; strict validation of
      all numeric options (no more atoi()).
    - Fix: pthread_create failures leaked connections or joined
      uninitialised thread ids; monitor join is guarded by a started
      flag; EMFILE/ENFILE in accept() now backs off instead of
      hot-spinning.
    - Fix: wildcard binds (empty host) tried IPv6-only first and became
      unreachable on IPv4; wildcards are now dual-stack.
    - Improvement: correct TCP half-close semantics, TCP_NODELAY on
      proxied connections, separate up/down UDP buckets, duplicate
      listener detection, 512 kB thread stacks, bounded rate-limit
      sleep slices, CRLF-tolerant config parser, unknown-key warnings.
    - Hardened build: PIE, full RELRO, FORTIFY_SOURCE=2,
      stack-protector-strong, format-security.
  * Initial Debian packaging: systemd unit (disabled by default),
    man page, default configuration in /etc/ratelimit/.

 -- Alejandro M. <ale@manalejandro.com>  Fri, 30 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
